  "live_records": {
    "q-51770": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51770",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194111/assets/1d1712fb97a1a508a5603e055218a863eb314310a8af74cf6344a3cd7ab50731.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$",
      "worked_solution": "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $0.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=0.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ becomes more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
