    "q-51769": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51769",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194111/assets/c0da20fd092718bf477d9b00ee86f953c72c130e7da20ebbe9ae0fdb47705102.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find $a$ and $b$ such that $f(x)\\to a$ as $x\\to \\infty $ and $f(x)\\to b$ as $x\\to -\\infty .$",
      "worked_solution": "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $b=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
