    "q-159681": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159681",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194111/assets/bdf61d89183886da1f519bad64221e7fdafd16a9ae5eb2c588728398f10477b5.png)\n\nWhat are the equations of the horizontal asymptotes of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ becomes larger and larger without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nThis does not give us a horizontal asymptote.\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ gets more and more negative without bound. We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nThis does not give us a horizontal asymptote.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-159681-a",
