    "q-77732": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77732",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194111/assets/f22c95e94846c80b0e1f6c0dc340da701f601a50a9a8908cde78d57825f38c89.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-1.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -1\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-1.$",
